    tldr: "For cancer that has spread widely to bone and causes pain everywhere at once, a bone-seeking radioactive injection travels to every affected spot in the skeleton and delivers pain-relieving radiation from the inside, sparing a trip to radiotherapy for each site.",
    summary: "Widespread osteoblastic (bone-forming) metastases, most often from prostate or breast cancer, can cause diffuse skeletal pain that is impractical to treat site-by-site with external beam radiotherapy. Systemically administered beta-emitting bone-seeking radiopharmaceuticals - strontium-89 chloride, samarium-153 lexidronam (153Sm-EDTMP) and, historically, phosphorus-32 sodium phosphate - concentrate at sites of increased osteoblastic turnover in proportion to bone remodelling activity, verified beforehand by a positive radionuclide bone scan, and deliver local beta radiation to relieve pain over one to a few weeks. The alpha-emitting radium-223 dichloride is a related but distinct case: as a calcium mimetic it also concentrates in osteoblastic bone lesions, but it is FDA- and EMA-approved specifically for castration-resistant prostate cancer with symptomatic bone metastases and no visceral disease, and, unlike the older beta emitters, it prolongs overall survival, not just pain.\n\nThe SNMMI/EANM procedure standard for these agents recommends 89Sr-chloride at roughly 148 MBq (or 1.5-2.2 MBq/kg), 153Sm-EDTMP at 37 MBq/kg, and 223Ra-dichloride at 55 kBq/kg every four weeks for six cycles; all three are given as a slow intravenous injection with attention to avoiding extravasation. Only 223Ra-dichloride has shown a survival benefit (from the ALSYMPCA trial); 89Sr and 153Sm are recommended solely for pain palliation and are not associated with any demonstrated survival advantage, and neither should be given to patients with a life expectancy under about four weeks because of a lag of days to weeks before pain relief.\n\nSelection is straightforward but firm: patients need painful lesions that are osteoblastic (not lytic) and bone-scan-avid, adequate baseline blood counts because myelosuppression is the dose-limiting toxicity of the beta emitters in particular, and no impending spinal cord compression or pathological fracture risk, which need surgery or external beam radiotherapy instead. Concomitant bisphosphonates are not contraindicated. Weekly complete blood counts are recommended for eight weeks after treatment, or until recovery from the nadir, given the risk of cytopenias.",
